    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "When you are working on those customer tickets, do you find that you have a hard time staying focused on the task until it is completed, or is it mostly just the careless mistakes that happen?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Well, it's not just the mistakes, honestly. Staying focused is... it's a real struggle. Especially when things drag on. Like, if they pull us into one of those longer coaching meetings, or if I have to read through a new procedure manual. I'll start off okay, but after a few minutes, I'll realize my eyes are just moving across the page, or I'm looking right at the supervisor, but I haven't taken in a single word they said for the last five minutes. \n\nThose coaching meetings, they always hold them in Conference Room B. I don't know who designed that room, but the fluorescent lights in there have this awful hum. It's like a low-grade buzzing right in your ear. It reminds me of the refrigerator in my first apartment. That thing used to vibrate so loud it would rattle the ice cube trays. I lived above a bakery back then, right on Main Street. The smell of fresh bread at 4:00 AM was incredible, but the baker, Tony, he'd play opera music while he worked. Full blast. Just Pavarotti echoing up through the floorboards while my fridge buzzed along with it. Tony made these amazing cannolis, though. Real ricotta, none of that cheap filler. \n\nBut yeah, when I'm reading those procedures or in those meetings, my attention just drifts off completely, and I have to go back and re-read the same paragraph three times just to figure out what I'm supposed to be doing.",
